 \section{Questions a reader may ask}\label{app:faq}
-\textbf{Is GPT A22 simply a worse model?} Per training token, yes (Table~\ref{tab:results}). Per GPU-second its difference from AR-ref is not resolved (300\,s, E1) while it is \CRParamReduction\% smaller, trains \CRNineTrainRatio$\times$ as fast and, on the original 256-token benchmark, decodes \CRDecodeRatio$\times$ faster with \CRMemoryReduction\% less inference memory. Which matters depends on whether compute time or data is the scarce resource.
+\textbf{Is GPT A22 simply a worse model?} Per training token, yes (Table~\ref{tab:results}). Per GPU-second its difference from AR-ref is not resolved (300\,s, in E1 and in the 13-recipe test) while it is \CRParamReduction\% smaller, trains \CRSixGptTrainRatio$\times$ as fast and decodes \CRSixGptDecRatio$\times$ as fast with \CRSixGptMemRed\% less inference memory (Table~\ref{tab:verify}). Which matters depends on whether compute time or data is the scarce resource.
 
 \textbf{Why not use a fixed-token contract from the start?} A fixed-time contract is the autoresearch standard and answers a practical question: the best model from a fixed amount of GPU time. It is not wrong, but it scores a program's throughput and its per-token quality together. A fixed-token contract isolates per-token quality but lets compute and update count vary. We report both.
 
-\textbf{Why did every agent shrink the model?} Under 300\,s, BPB improves with more updates and tokens, and a smaller model gets more of both. The agents' hypotheses say so (Table~\ref{tab:reasoning}). E1 prices the trade for GPT A22: it trains \CREOneRho$\times$ faster but needs about \CREOneK$\times$ AR-ref's tokens for equal quality, so at 300\,s the two roughly cancel. Opus's recipes would need more than \CRMTKBound$\times$ AR-ref's tokens, about their speed advantage, and post hoc, at 201.3M tokens they do not differ resolvably from AR-ref at 300\,s (95\% CIs span zero).
+\textbf{Why did every agent shrink the model?} Under 300\,s, BPB improves with more updates and tokens, and a smaller model gets more of both. The agents' hypotheses say so (Table~\ref{tab:reasoning}). E1 prices the trade for GPT A22: it trains \CREOneRho$\times$ faster but needs about \CREOneK$\times$ AR-ref's tokens for equal quality, so at 300\,s the two roughly cancel. Opus's recipes would need more than \CRMTKBound$\times$ AR-ref's tokens, about their speed advantage, and in the pre-registered 13-recipe test they do not differ resolvably from AR-ref at 300\,s (95\% CIs span zero; Table~\ref{tab:paired6}).
+
+\textbf{Does a 201.3M-token budget favour the larger model?} It is about 2.3$\times$ AR-ref's own 300\,s exposure and within 3\% of Opus's. E1 also ran 88.6M and 157.3M tokens, the first matching GPT A22's discovery exposure, and GPT A22 is worse at every budget (Figure~\ref{fig:e1}). The other recipes ran one matched budget, so their gap's dependence on it is untested.
 
 \textbf{Does E1 prove that exposure is the only cause?} No. E1 shows that at equal tokens and updates GPT A22 is worse, on the same seeds and data, and its exploratory split attributes the 300\,s result to extra tokens offsetting a weaker architecture. Tokens and updates move together, and architecture and optimizer edits are bundled, so finer causes are not separated.
 
-\textbf{Are three seeds enough?} For the matched-token gap, yes: \CREOneMatchedLoAbs--\CREOneMatchedHiAbs{} held-out BPB against a within-recipe seed SD of at most \CREOneMaxSD, on all \CREOnePairs{} seed--budget pairs, and the panel result replicates on other seeds and another A40. For the slope of the gap with tokens, or to show equivalence at 300\,s, no.
+\textbf{Are three seeds enough?} For the matched-token gap, yes: \CREOneMatchedLoAbs--\CREOneMatchedHiAbs{} held-out BPB against a within-recipe seed SD of at most \CREOneMaxSD, on all \CREOnePairs{} seed--budget pairs, and the panel result replicates on other seeds and another A40. In the 13-recipe test all \CRSixTokWorseCount{} matched-token gaps to AR-ref are resolved, each worse on all three seeds (Table~\ref{tab:paired6}). For the slope of the gap with tokens, or to show equivalence at 300\,s, no.
 
-\textbf{Did Opus game the decode metric?} It stayed within the permitted surface: the adapter had to compute the same outputs, and every variant passed the correctness checks with identical greedy tokens on the 64- and 256-token prefixes they cover. Those checks reach neither the timed window nor the 1{,}024-token ring cache, so full-workload equivalence is not established. Its records describe graph replay as a fix for launch-bound decode and note the prefill cost (Table~\ref{tab:reasoning}), so it was the metric's definition, not a rule violation, that rewarded the placement. We report decode-only and end-to-end speed side by side and recommend prefill-inclusive scoring.
